WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:06.720
Twitter is lying to you about what people&nbsp;
watch, and I’m going to prove it to you.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.420
That’s it. That’s the tweet.
Hello Internet! Welcome to Film Theory,

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.640
the show that won’t make you pay eight dollars&nbsp;
for a fake blue checkmark. Pop quiz Theorists:

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.700
What’s the most watched show on television&nbsp;
right now? Go on down into the comments,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.300
make yourself a guess. And when I reveal the&nbsp;
answer later this episode, go and give that

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.119
comment an edit to let me know if you were&nbsp;
right. No shame in wrong answers by the way,

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:33.240
because let me tell you, I did not know. If you&nbsp;
had given me ten guesses, I would have never

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.640
gotten it. I had never heard of this thing before,&nbsp;
but apparently it has sequels and spinoffs,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.980
and NO ONE on the team has ever watched a fraction&nbsp;
of a single episode. Anyway, I’ll give you the

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.464
answer later in this episode because that’s where&nbsp;
the story ends. Here’s where it begins

00:00:48.464 --> 00:00:51.329
"So what does her heartbeat sound like? Mighty."

00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:54.720
Did you guys see the new trailer&nbsp;
for Avatar: The Way of Water? I did,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.740
but again, that’s because it’s my job to try&nbsp;
and stay on top of these things. Without that,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:04.920
I honestly doubt I would have. I have not heard&nbsp;
anyone talking about this movie online. And that

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.260
immediately interested me considering this is the&nbsp;
sequel to literally the highest grossing film of

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:15.300
all time. In case you weren’t around or paying&nbsp;
attention 13 years ago when it came out in 2009,

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.160
Avatar was a HUGE deal. It was James&nbsp;
Cameron’s first movie since his previous

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.737
highest-grossing film of all time: Titanic.

00:01:22.737 --> 00:01:26.286
"I'm the king of the world!"

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:28.200
Not the most humble guy that one. I wonder

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.400
if I could get him to overpay for a social media&nbsp;
platform that’s hemorrhaging money like an open

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.400
wound… Anyway, Avatar was basically Pocahontas&nbsp;
meets the Blue Man Group in space. And yet,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.840
despite the only significant challenge to this&nbsp;
film’s status as “highest grossing film of all

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.880
time” coming in the form of the biggest crossover&nbsp;
in cinematic history, I haven’t seen a peep about

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:53.280
its sequel over on Twitter. In fact, it’s trended&nbsp;
only twice this year, which is the same number of

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.200
times that I trended, and ah, not to undersell&nbsp;
myself or anything but let’s be honest, I am not

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.660
the sequel to the biggest movie in history that&nbsp;
made $2.9 billion at the box office. For context,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:09.420
it’d only take about 14.6 Avatars to pay for&nbsp;
Twitter. A solid conversion rate to be sure.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.880
And this sequel is coming out…IN DECEMBER? Hold&nbsp;
on, that soon? Seriously, what is going on here?

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.560
Because my analytics brain knew that Avatar did&nbsp;
super well, the lack of Twitter chatter actually

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.180
made me curious enough to look deeper into it.&nbsp;
Maybe people just don’t care about this movie.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:29.160
But no, that doesn’t seem to be the case at&nbsp;
all. Avatar 2 has been tracking super well.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.420
According to The Quorum, a website that compiles&nbsp;
pre-release film data, The Way of Water has been

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:38.040
tracking consistently high in awareness, interest,&nbsp;
whether people will actually pay to see it,

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.320
and if they’ll see it in theaters. Basically, a&nbsp;
ton of people already know about Avatar 2 and they

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.640
are willing to pay to see it on the big screen.&nbsp;
In fact, Avatar 2 has been ahead of Black Panther:

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:54.000
Wakanda Forever for the entirety of the year, only&nbsp;
falling into second place the week that Black Panther 2

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.900
came out. All of this while the marketing for&nbsp;
the movie has barely gotten beyond a trailer

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:03.480
or two. Honestly, looking at these numbers had me&nbsp;
baffled. Was the tracking data just wrong? Turns

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:09.000
out, no. In truth, what goes viral online isn’t&nbsp;
necessarily what’s actually popular. And the worst

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.460
culprit perpetuating all of this is Twitter. What&nbsp;
is talked about on Twitter is not reflective of

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.720
what people are actually talking about out in the&nbsp;
real world. If you actually look at the numbers,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.160
Twitter is borderline lying to you about&nbsp;
what people actually care about in their TV

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.900
and film media, and I’m about to prove it to you.
To start things off here, let’s put some things

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:33.000
into perspective. We forget just how small a&nbsp;
platform Twitter actually is. In 2022, it’s

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:39.540
estimated that Twitter has 396.5 million users.&nbsp;
If we’re unrealistically generous to Twitter and

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:44.580
assume that none of them are bots or people with&nbsp;
multiple accounts, that means just about 5% of the

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.960
world’s population has a twitter account. It's&nbsp;
not too shabby, don’t get me wrong. But, for

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.780
comparison's sake, let's just put that up against&nbsp;
YouTube. YouTube has an estimated 2.6 billion

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:59.580
monthly active users, and if we again assume&nbsp;
there are no bots or users with multiple accounts,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:05.700
that means that just under a THIRD of the world’s&nbsp;
population uses YouTube every month. Twitter's

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:11.700
396.5 Million figure is their entire userbase, not&nbsp;
just the people who are using it at least once a

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:15.960
month. And believe it or not, YouTube isn’t even&nbsp;
the biggest fish in this sea; other social media

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.880
is just as huge, if not bigger. TikTok has&nbsp;
itself an estimated 1 billion monthly active

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:28.260
users. Instagram has 1.4 billion, and Facebook&nbsp;
has an insane 2.9 billion. Heck, even Pinterest and

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:34.020
Reddit are bigger than Twitter, with 480 million&nbsp;
and 430 million monthly active users respectively.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:40.920
What’s more, a study from November of 2021 found&nbsp;
that just 25% of Twitter’s userbase generates 97%

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:47.040
of the website's content. That translates to fewer&nbsp;
than 100 million people dictating almost all the

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.200
conversation on Twitter, and those voices are&nbsp;
massively overrepresented by journalists and

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.460
media personalities who use Twitter to source and&nbsp;
break news. I mean, just how many articles have

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:01.020
you seen claiming that “PEOPLE ARE FURIOUS ABOUT&nbsp;
THING”, and then you click on it and all they’re doing

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:06.780
is citing random tweets from random accounts. No&nbsp;
actual facts or trends or data, just the opinion

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:12.360
of a very small slice of one or two random tweets.&nbsp;
What’s more, what users actually see on twitter

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:16.860
is very tailored to their own interests. Just&nbsp;
like a lot of Social Media, Twitter’s algorithms

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:21.540
create echo chambers that only show you the things&nbsp;
you actively engage with. So, while you might

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:25.320
think you’re getting shown a bunch of new and&nbsp;
different ideas and topics all the time, you’re

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.160
actually just getting fed things that Twitter thinks&nbsp;
that you’re gonna like and follow. For example,

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:34.500
if you’re really into the MCU, you’re going to get&nbsp;
served a lot of MCU content in your browsing… and

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.580
also a lot of Guilty Gear. Twitter really thinks&nbsp;
I’m going to be into Guilty Gear for some reason.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:43.320
And the thing is, that vocal minority on Twitter&nbsp;
is driving a lot of the decision-making that’s

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.340
happening in and around Hollywood. Sometimes the&nbsp;
changes are for the better, like when Sonic the

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.511
Hedgehog’s design got completely overhauled&nbsp;
thanks to the social media backlash against

00:05:51.511 --> 00:05:55.860
Ugly Sonic. But, sometimes they’re for the worse.&nbsp;
Like when Star Wars crumbled under the social

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:01.920
media pressure brought upon by The Last Jedi to make&nbsp;
the confusing, unfocused and apologetic mess that

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.940
was The Rise of Skywalker. And we’re not just&nbsp;
talking about the decisions inside the movies

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.440
themselves that are being affected by online&nbsp;
discourse, but what gets greenlit in the first

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:15.360
place. The fantasy book series Eragon just got&nbsp;
picked up by Disney thanks, specifically, to a

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.280
fandom surge on Twitter. Daredevil actor Charlie&nbsp;
Cox has repeatedly said that the #SaveDaredevil

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:24.420
Twitter campaign saved his career after corporate&nbsp;
disagreement got the show canceled on Netflix.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:28.980
Morbius was re-released in theaters because&nbsp;
clearly everyone memeing the thing on Twitter

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:34.080
wanted a chance to Morb all over the theater&nbsp;
again. And, of course, there’s the Snyder Cut of

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.580
Justice League, where heads of the company went on&nbsp;
record to say, quote: “Since I got here 14 months

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:43.500
ago, the chant to #ReleaseTheSnyderCut has been a&nbsp;
daily drumbeat in our offices and inboxes. Well,

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:47.460
the fans have asked, and we are thrilled to&nbsp;
finally deliver.” The ironic part of all of

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:51.840
that is in the aftermath, it came out the whole&nbsp;
#ReleaseTheSnyderCut campaign was likely driven

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.280
by Snyder himself and an army of bots Just&nbsp;
to get fans riled up as an act of revenge

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:01.320
against Warner Brothers. Regardless, those&nbsp;
are just a few examples of how this relatively

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:07.080
small social media platform made up of a VERY&nbsp;
vocal minority has historically had a huge sway

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.720
in the entertainment industry.
So, with all of that in mind,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:14.160
we wanted to find out just how skewed these&nbsp;
results can get. We needed a way to compare

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:17.820
what people were actually talking about on&nbsp;
Twitter to what was actually being watched

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.540
beyond the Twittersphere. Now, finding the data&nbsp;
about what was trending on Twitter is actually

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:26.040
pretty easy. Several websites have archived&nbsp;
that exact information by time and date. But,

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.000
finding exactly what to compare it against is&nbsp;
kind of difficult, you can’t exactly just look

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.200
at box office numbers to gauge success anymore.&nbsp;
Getting big bucks at theaters is great, don’t

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:39.540
get me wrong. It’s just not the only metric that&nbsp;
matters anymore. So we turned to Parrot Analytics,

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:44.100
a data company who made their name with a software&nbsp;
that's able to track the “true demand” for movies,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:49.500
TV, and digital media. They do this by running a&nbsp;
complex algorithm that takes a lot of variables

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:54.240
into account: How many people watched? In what&nbsp;
demographics? For how long? How does this product

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.560
trend in search results? Where does it fall in the&nbsp;
news cycle? Even stuff like: How much money are

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.060
people spending on this thing? And, what is the&nbsp;
tone of the search results and news coverage? Now

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:08.340
that is a lot of data to throw into an alchemical&nbsp;
analytics concoction, and some of it did make me

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:12.660
squint my eyes suspiciously. But, you know what?&nbsp;
I think that it offers a valuable insight into

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:17.100
what people are watching and it can act as a kind&nbsp;
of a North Star for our analysis here today.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:22.560
Looking at their Top 10 list of Most In Demand&nbsp;
Overall Movie as of July 2022, the results were

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.420
eye-opening to put it mildly. Sure, a lot of the&nbsp;
list is what you would expect: Spider-Man at the

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:32.940
top? Absolutely expected. The Batman in the top&nbsp;
five? Sure, because Batman. But then you had some

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:38.220
surprises. Venom: Let There be Carnage earned&nbsp;
the number two most in-demand spot. That tracks

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:42.720
considering it was the third highest grossing&nbsp;
film of 2021. But the day it was released, Venom&nbsp;&nbsp;2

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:49.920
trended for only 90 minutes. It only got itself&nbsp;
11,850 tweets. Now compare that to the number one

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:57.180
spot of Spider-Man… the day No Way Home released,&nbsp;
Spider-Man trended with a maximum 398,000

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:01.920
tweets. Its longest trend streak ran&nbsp;
for seven and a half hours. Meanwhile,

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:09.720
Marvel AND Tom Holland were also breaking into&nbsp;
the most trending tab, with 182,000 and 131,500

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:14.820
tweets respectively. Venom 2 was a TENTH of&nbsp;
those. No one was talking about this thing

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:19.980
over on Twitter, despite it being one of the most&nbsp;
successful, highest earning movies of last year.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:24.420
Meanwhile, Zack Snyder’s Justice League&nbsp;
shows the exact opposite pattern. On the

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:30.000
day it was announced, some variation of “Snyder”&nbsp;
or “Snyder Cut” peaked on trending with 170,000

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.040
tweets and a streak of seven hours. On the&nbsp;
day it was released it peaked with 332,000

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:41.160
tweets and had an insane streak of 19 hours. I&nbsp;
mean, that’s on par with Spider-Man numbers. And

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.720
yet despite all of this online chatter and&nbsp;
this massive multi-month long fan campaign,

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:51.000
it was only watched by only 2.2 million homes&nbsp;
the first week with reports noting that just

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:56.040
36% of people who watched it actually finished it.
The last one I’ll call out for now is The

00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:00.960
Eternals. On the day of its release, Eternals&nbsp;
trended on Twitter with just over 99,000 tweets,

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.720
and a streak of 4.5 hours. Not bad by any means,&nbsp;
but nowhere close to Black Widow, which trended

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:13.062
with nearly double the amount of tweets, 193,000,&nbsp;
and a streak of 7.5 hours on its release date

00:10:13.062 --> 00:10:18.600
(July 9th, 2021). And yet, Eternals managed to&nbsp;
out-perform its rival MCU release in all metrics.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:22.560
In short, Twitter’s trends just don’t seem to&nbsp;
consistently align with what people are actually

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:27.000
watching, what people actually care about. Which&nbsp;
brings us back to the question that I started

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:31.620
off with: what is the actual biggest show on&nbsp;
TV right now? Believe it or not, according

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:36.660
to Nielsen Ratings, the most watched show on&nbsp;
television right now by a HUGE margin is this

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:42.540
series called Yellowstone. It’s a gritty drama&nbsp;
about modern cowboys and rural ranch politics with

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:47.400
lots and lots of fights. The report claims&nbsp;
that 13% of all households watching TV while

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:53.640
Yellowstone was on were watching the show. This&nbsp;
translated to a staggering 9.3 million viewers

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:59.340
during its Season 4 finale in January of 2022.&nbsp;
Literally the only thing that beats Yellowstone

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:05.400
on broadcast TV is football. The ah, handegg one,&nbsp;
not the European “futbol”. Even more incredibly,

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:10.680
the show doesn’t even air on one of the mainstream&nbsp;
channels like NBC or CBS. Instead, it’s on the

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:15.900
Paramount Network, which used to be called Spike&nbsp;
TV, home of CSI reruns and Deadliest Warrior.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:20.050
While digging into this data at first, I thought&nbsp;
these viewership numbers had to be juiced in

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:24.660
some way. The “views” coming out of TV Ratings&nbsp;
can sometimes be inflated, so I had to triple

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:29.220
check myself. But apparently, the series and&nbsp;
its creator have made so much money for Paramount

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:34.320
that they’re going all in on the Yellowstone&nbsp;
IP. It’s spun off into a prequel called 1883,

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.580
which itself is getting a spinoff about&nbsp;
the real-life Western hero Bass Reeves,

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:43.560
it’s also getting a Prequel Sequel called&nbsp;
1923 starring Harrison Ford and Helen Mirren,

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:49.620
and another contemporary spinoff called 6666 set&nbsp;
in Texas instead of Montana. There’s basically

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:55.800
an entire Yellowstone Cinematic Universe, and no&nbsp;
one on Team Theorist knew about this thing. NOT

00:11:55.800 --> 00:12:00.420
ONE! But you know who did know about it? Our&nbsp;
parents. People that aren’t on Twitter. Yea,

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:04.200
one of our team members actually asked their&nbsp;
mom if they watched Yellowstone and got this

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:08.820
text message back: “LOVE IT!!! Cowboys&nbsp;
are cute.” Apparently everyone they knew would

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:13.020
watch it and talk about it. Even the dogs would&nbsp;
bark whenever a horse would show up on screen.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:19.260
And, as you might expect, looking at Google Trends&nbsp;
data, the show crushes. Not even Black Panther can

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:23.940
catch it the week of its release. But now, look&nbsp;
at the Twitter data from when that finale aired

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:29.382
back in January. Practically nothing. It barely&nbsp;
broke into Twitter's trending list, maxing out at

00:12:29.382 --> 00:12:35.340
13,000 tweets with a longest streak of just&nbsp;
3.5 hours. Basically, this was barely a blip on

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:39.840
Twitter’s radar. There seems to be this massive&nbsp;
audience of apparent Boomers and older Gen-Xers

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:43.855
that is really underrepresented on Twitter, and&nbsp;
therefore the resulting trends that people talk

00:12:43.855 --> 00:12:48.960
about on YouTube and Twitch and whatever. Really,&nbsp;
this sort of silent majority when it comes to what

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:53.160
people are actually consuming is fascinating.&nbsp;
Probably deserves its own Theory at some point.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:57.120
In short, as Twitter teeters on the verge of&nbsp;
collapse right now, I wanted to make this episode

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.380
to point out the echo chamber that it keeps a lot&nbsp;
of its users in. And the false perception that it

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:06.600
gives about the world of content around us. I&nbsp;
think a lot of its users assumed that this was the

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:10.920
new “water cooler,” the neutral place that would&nbsp;
serve as an accurate representation of everything

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:15.660
happening around us, a place where people would go&nbsp;
and gather to chat about their favorite TV shows,

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:20.580
movies, sports, politics, whatever. But no.&nbsp;
It is not that. It feeds you what it thinks

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:24.000
you want to hear and prevents you from learning&nbsp;
about things that are important to the people

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:28.620
around you. Sometimes those things are trivial,&nbsp;
like a show about hunky cowboys, but oftentimes,

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:32.880
it’s stuff that’s a whole lot more important.&nbsp;
And so, regardless of the platform’s future,

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:37.620
I’d warn you to not fall into the trap that the&nbsp;
Disneys and Warner Brothers did, making decisions

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:43.440
off a few tweets coming from a very vocal subset&nbsp;
of the most extreme users, or worse, basing

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:48.720
your opinions off of an army of bots created by&nbsp;
deep-pocketed users with ulterior motives. Now,

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:53.040
if you’ll excuse me, I’ve gotta go see how Rip’s&nbsp;
sheriff investigation goes after his accident

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:58.200
with those tourists. It’s a Yellowstone thing.&nbsp;
You wouldn’t understand…or maybe, now you will.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:05.100
But hey, that’s just a Theory!&nbsp;
A Film Theory! Aaaaaaand cut.

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:25.020
foreign

